1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,886
Now the lab:

2
00:00:00,886 --> 00:00:06,648
apply transformations and scaling, and compare distributions before and after.

3
00:00:07,448 --> 00:00:08,040
Step one:

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:19,688
load the California Housing data, fill the two hundred seven missing total bedrooms values with the median, because the column is skewed, and select the seven positive features.

5
00:00:19,688 --> 00:00:23,768
Transformers cannot handle missing values, so fill them first.

6
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:25,215
Step two:

7
00:00:25,215 --> 00:00:32,690
measure skew, kurtosis and I Q R outliers for all seven features in one data frame, and draw histograms.

8
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:36,211
Housing median age is symmetric with no outliers.

9
00:00:36,211 --> 00:00:47,855
Total rooms, bedrooms, population and households have skews of three to five and kurtosis from twenty two up to seventy three, with over a thousand outliers each.

10
00:00:47,855 --> 00:00:51,592
Median income and house value are moderately skewed.

11
00:00:52,392 --> 00:00:53,172
Step three:

12
00:00:53,172 --> 00:00:57,144
log transform all features at once with numpy log one p.

13
00:00:57,144 --> 00:01:02,890
Strong right skew of about four flips to a moderate left skew of about minus one:

14
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:05,798
log is too strong for the count features.

15
00:01:05,798 --> 00:01:09,274
Median income and house value land close to zero.

16
00:01:09,274 --> 00:01:16,296
And housing median age, which was symmetric at zero point oh six, becomes minus one point one four.

17
00:01:17,096 --> 00:01:17,849
Step four:

18
00:01:17,849 --> 00:01:20,560
Box-Cox with one lambda per feature.

19
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:24,175
For one column, stats boxcox returns the lambda.

20
00:01:24,175 --> 00:01:33,589
For all seven columns in one command, use Power Transformer with method box-cox and standardize false, then read the lambdas.

21
00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:38,559
Every feature now has absolute skew of at most zero point one two.

22
00:01:38,559 --> 00:01:47,823
Box-Cox picked a weak power of point eight one for the symmetric age feature and a strong one, about point one, for income.

23
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:49,856
It adapts where log cannot.

24
00:01:50,656 --> 00:01:53,464
Here is the skewness of every feature:

25
00:01:53,464 --> 00:01:55,015
raw, log and Box-Cox.

26
00:01:55,015 --> 00:02:03,733
The raw bars reach five, the log bars overshoot below zero, and the Box-Cox bars sit near zero for all seven features.

27
00:02:03,733 --> 00:02:09,496
The kurtosis of population falls from seventy three point six to two point oh.

28
00:02:10,296 --> 00:02:12,773
One feature, three shapes:

29
00:02:12,773 --> 00:02:13,821
population.

30
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:16,584
Raw is one spike at the left.

31
00:02:16,584 --> 00:02:19,823
Log gives a bell with a left tail.

32
00:02:19,823 --> 00:02:22,872
Box-Cox is closest to symmetric.

33
00:02:23,672 --> 00:02:27,624
Density of all seven features, raw versus Box-Cox.

34
00:02:27,624 --> 00:02:32,526
Both are standardised only so their shapes can share one axis.

35
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:36,320
Peaks drop and right tails shrink after Box-Cox.

36
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:40,325
Outliers before and after, by the I Q R rule.

37
00:02:40,325 --> 00:02:45,596
Median house value outliers fall from one thousand seventy one to sixteen:

38
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:47,733
almost all were just the tail.

39
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:54,643
But logging the symmetric housing median age invented five hundred fifty nine outliers from zero.

40
00:02:54,643 --> 00:02:59,344
Transformations should be chosen per feature, not applied blindly.

41
00:03:00,144 --> 00:03:00,901
Step five:

42
00:03:00,901 --> 00:03:02,716
apply the three scalers.

43
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:09,147
Standard scaler, min-max scaler and robust scaler, each in one line over all columns.

44
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:13,156
For population, the raw mean is fourteen twenty five.

45
00:03:13,156 --> 00:03:26,850
After standard scaling the mean is zero and the standard deviation one; after min-max the values are between zero and one; after robust scaling the median is zero and the I Q R one.

46
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:30,027
Skew is four point nine four in every row:

47
00:03:30,027 --> 00:03:32,448
scaling never changes the shape.

48
00:03:33,248 --> 00:03:35,954
The same shape with different units:

49
00:03:35,954 --> 00:03:37,007
median income.

50
00:03:37,007 --> 00:03:44,600
The four histograms are identical, only the x-axis numbers change, and skew stays one point six five.

51
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:48,583
Box plots of all features after each scaler.

52
00:03:48,583 --> 00:03:55,311
With standard scaling, features are centred but count features still reach fifteen to thirty.

53
00:03:55,311 --> 00:03:59,724
With min-max scaling, count-feature boxes collapse near zero.

54
00:03:59,724 --> 00:04:05,728
With robust scaling, every box has median zero and height one, directly comparable.

55
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:07,760
The min-max trap:

56
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:10,151
one extreme value sets the scale.

57
00:04:10,151 --> 00:04:16,528
Ninety six percent of population values fall below zero point one after min-max scaling.

58
00:04:16,528 --> 00:04:27,470
The largest block group, thirty five thousand people, becomes one, while a typical block group of eleven hundred sixty six becomes zero point oh three.

59
00:04:27,470 --> 00:04:32,977
The top four percent of rows occupy ninety percent of the zero-to-one range.

60
00:04:32,977 --> 00:04:33,557
The fix:

61
00:04:33,557 --> 00:04:36,600
transform first, or use the robust scaler.

62
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,086
Step six:

63
00:04:38,086 --> 00:04:40,221
transform first, then scale.

64
00:04:40,221 --> 00:04:48,683
Power Transformer with Box-Cox and the default standardize true does Box-Cox plus standard scaling in one step.

65
00:04:48,683 --> 00:04:56,154
Every feature has mean zero, standard deviation one, and absolute skew at most zero point one two.

66
00:04:56,154 --> 00:05:00,804
The population maximum falls from thirty to seven point five.

67
00:05:00,804 --> 00:05:08,504
For models, fit on training data only, then transform the test data, so no test information leaks in.

68
00:05:09,304 --> 00:05:10,808
Which method when?

69
00:05:10,808 --> 00:05:14,568
Strong right skew with all values above zero:

70
00:05:14,568 --> 00:05:15,236
Box-Cox.

71
00:05:15,236 --> 00:05:19,915
Right skew with zeros allowed and a wish for simplicity:

72
00:05:19,915 --> 00:05:20,750
log one p.

73
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:22,338
Zeros or negatives:

74
00:05:22,338 --> 00:05:23,340
Yeo-Johnson.

75
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:24,844
Already symmetric:

76
00:05:24,844 --> 00:05:30,359
no transform; log on age created five hundred fifty nine outliers.

77
00:05:30,359 --> 00:05:34,620
Roughly normal data for P C A, regression or S V M:

78
00:05:34,620 --> 00:05:35,956
standard scaler.

79
00:05:35,956 --> 00:05:38,881
Need zero to one with few outliers:

80
00:05:38,881 --> 00:05:39,549
min-max.

81
00:05:39,549 --> 00:05:41,972
Many outliers or heavy tails:

82
00:05:41,972 --> 00:05:43,142
robust scaler.

83
00:05:43,142 --> 00:05:49,408
And tree models usually need none, since splits depend on order, not scale.

84
00:05:50,208 --> 00:05:54,073
Transform the shape, then scale the units.

85
00:05:54,073 --> 00:05:56,558
Check skew before choosing.

86
00:05:56,558 --> 00:05:59,227
Box-Cox adapts, log does not.

87
00:05:59,227 --> 00:06:01,528
Scaling never fixes skew.

88
00:06:01,528 --> 00:06:05,670
And robust beats min-max when outliers exist.

89
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:09,535
The lab notebook has exercises at the end.

90
00:06:09,535 --> 00:06:11,376
Thanks for watching.

